# Classification / Odd One Out — In-Depth Guide — Part 4 of 16

[info title="Part 4 of 16"]
Continuing from the previous part. back to Part 3 (`classification-detailed-part-3`) · continue to Part 5 (`classification-detailed-part-5`).
[/info]

## 42. Letter Count Classification

```text
APPLE
MANGO
GRAPE
BANANA
```

Count vowels:

```text
APPLE  → 2
MANGO  → 2
GRAPE  → 2
BANANA → 3
```

**Answer: BANANA**

---

## 43. Repeated-Letter Classification

```text
APPLE
MANGO
LEVEL
GRAPE
```

LEVEL has repeated letters.

APPLE also has P repeated, so this would **not** be a valid classification if the rule is simply "contains repeated letters."

This demonstrates an important principle:

> **Always test the rule against every option.**

---

## 44. Unique-Letter Classification

Example:

```text
DOG
CAT
SUN
APPLE
```

APPLE contains repeated P.

The others contain no repeated letter.

**Answer: APPLE**

---

## 45. Alphabetical Order Classification

```text
ABC
DEF
GHI
ZXY
```

ABC, DEF and GHI are alphabetically increasing.

ZXY is not.

**Answer: ZXY**

---

## 46. Reverse Alphabetical Order

```text
CBA
FED
IHG
JKL
```

CBA, FED and IHG are descending.

JKL is ascending.

**Answer: JKL**

---

## 47. Word Semantic Classification

```text
Happy
Joyful
Cheerful
Angry
```

Three indicate happiness.

Angry indicates an opposite emotional state.

**Answer: Angry**

---

## 48. Synonym Classification

```text
Big
Large
Huge
Small
```

Big, large and huge are related by large size.

Small is opposite.

**Answer: Small**

---

## 49. Antonym Classification

```text
Hot
Cold
Warm
Heat
```

This needs careful interpretation. Classification should not be based on superficial similarity.

For example, if three represent temperature states and one represents an abstract action/object, the intended answer depends on the options and exact wording.

> **Lesson:** Do not force a rule when the group is ambiguous.

---

## 50. Gender Classification

```text
King
Queen
Prince
Princess
```

All are gendered royal titles, so this is **not automatically** an odd-one-out question.

The options must supply a clear distinction such as:

```text
King
Prince
Man
Queen
```

Then Queen may be the only female term.

---

## 51. Male/Female Classification

```text
King
Man
Boy
Queen
```

King, man and boy are male.

Queen is female.

**Answer: Queen**

---

## 52. Singular/Plural Classification

```text
Child
Man
Boy
Books
```

Child, man and boy are singular.

Books is plural.

**Answer: Books**

---

## 53. Living/Non-Living Classification

```text
Dog
Tree
Bird
Chair
```

Dog, tree and bird are living.

Chair is non-living.

**Answer: Chair**

---

## 54. Natural/Man-Made Classification

```text
River
Mountain
Forest
Bridge
```

River, mountain and forest are natural.

Bridge is man-made.

**Answer: Bridge**

---

## 55. Solid/Liquid/Gas Classification

```text
Iron
Water
Copper
Gold
```

Iron, copper and gold are solids.

Water is liquid.

**Answer: Water**

---

## 56. Conductors Classification

```text
Copper
Aluminium
Silver
Rubber
```

Copper, aluminium and silver conduct electricity well.

Rubber is an insulator.

**Answer: Rubber**

---

## 57. Planet Classification

```text
Earth
Mars
Venus
Moon
```

Earth, Mars and Venus are planets.

Moon is a natural satellite.

**Answer: Moon**

---
